{"offset": 0, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "69be111000f9a5c00915f12beb8ebeb51aad367d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02574", "CorpusId": 295501975}, "title": "RLVR Sharpens the Pencil, Not the Mind", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02574, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2525173592", "name": "Shreeyam Bangera"}, {"authorId": "2344751601", "name": "Manya Gupta"}], "abstract": "Models like DeepSeek-R1 made Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) famous: reward a model for correct answers and it gets dramatically better at reasoning without being taught how. The leading explanation is sharpening---that the base model already knows how to solve these problems and RLVR just makes it more likely to reach for its best solution paths. This has only ever been checked by comparing before and after training, never by watching what changes inside across it. We tracked OLMo-2-1B across all 13 training checkpoints on GSM8K. First-try accuracy climbs from 58.0% to 61.3%, but ask the model 256 times and solution coverage actually rises from 94.5% to 96.9%, proving the knowledge was already there. We then planted reasoning mistakes into problems and trained simple probes to detect them. Those probes held between AUROC 0.798 and 0.840 across all 13 checkpoints and 3 error types, a spread smaller than the confidence interval; the model never builds a sharper internal picture of what a reasoning mistake looks like. The one place we find real internal change is at the final answer token, where a probe distinguishing early from late checkpoints hits an AUROC of 0.93, while the same probe on the actual reasoning steps lands near chance. RLVR makes the model lead with its best thinking. It does not change what that thinking is."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dd40e11bd6dcb492bc55b22168b6473e430be12a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22046", "CorpusId": 293003633}, "title": "Effectiveness of Hidden-State Probes for Localizing Reasoning Failures", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22046, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2500606166", "name": "Shubham Subhnil"}, {"authorId": "2330190108", "name": "Yannis Belkhiter"}, {"authorId": "2278648904", "name": "Erik Miehling"}, {"authorId": "2059761581", "name": "Adam Dahlgren Lindstr\u00f6m"}, {"authorId": "2253674008", "name": "Elizabeth M. Daly"}], "abstract": "Verifying the final answer of a long reasoning trace may show that the trace is wrong but it does not distinguish three cases. The problem may lie beyond the model's capability, another attempt would succeed, or one step in an otherwise recoverable trace made the trace fail. Standard hidden-state probes are trained on final-answer correctness, a label that conflates these cases. Monte Carlo rollouts distinguish the cases and locate the failing step at additional inference cost. We test the hidden states of 4B and 8B reasoning models for the failure type and the failing step and compare each probe with the strongest baseline that uses no hidden states. On incorrect 8B traces, the confidence probe, a calibrated correctness probe at a midpoint cutoff, misses 81% of cases in which a single step makes the trace unrecoverable and 47% of cases in which another attempt would succeed. A probe with a different training design over the same states reverses the ordering of these two kinds of failure, so either ordering can be read from those states. At both scales, no single tested probe locates the failing step more accurately than a rule based only on trace length, and at 4B a probe trained to predict that step fails to outperform this rule in any of 10 runs on identical traces. The confidence probe therefore indicates that an answer is wrong, but its failure-type ordering depends on its training design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f8bd01568a7bc1bcfaac9751e2c857f691373345", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15412", "CorpusId": 292897119}, "title": "From Candidate Tokens to Final Outcomes: Decoding Relative Long-Horizon Candidate Value from Reasoning Model Representations", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15412, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2284730757", "name": "Yi-Xin Yang"}, {"authorId": "2266372096", "name": "Yue Wang"}, {"authorId": "33427918", "name": "Fan-Dong Meng"}, {"authorId": "2287829088", "name": "Zhi-Fang Sui"}], "abstract": "Large reasoning models generate reasoning trajectories token by token. At a given prefix, multiple next-token candidates may be plausible, yet shape subsequent reasoning and differ in their chances of leading to the correct answer. Whether these differences are reflected in internal representations remains unclear. To study this question, we define relative long-horizon candidate value to quantify within-prefix differences and construct the Reasoning Candidate-Token Value (RCTV) dataset, pairing mathematical reasoning prefixes with plausible next-token candidates and their empirical long-horizon candidate values. Using RCTV, we examine hidden states, multilayer perceptron outputs, and attention outputs at candidate positions with sparse linear probes. Experimental results provide evidence that relative long-horizon candidate value is linearly decodable from these representations at multiple layers. Candidate-value probes can also outperform probability- and embedding-based baselines. Furthermore, a controlled online intervention experiment shows that using the decoded signals to guide token selection during generation can improve the chances of eventual success. Our findings provide evidence that internal representations encode information about relative long-horizon candidate value, offering insights into how local candidate-level representations relate to long-horizon reasoning outcomes."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "23492d997e2a70e70a01115b4d7e8f7ba2a8bc5d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.34732", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34732", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34732", "CorpusId": 292435619}, "title": "What Visual Generators Need from Teachers: Rethinking Representation Alignment", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34732, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2292669235", "name": "Yong-Cong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2455267940", "name": "Hingchin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2396424791", "name": "Ming-Yu Fan"}, {"authorId": "2502587873", "name": "Shuo Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2505470938", "name": "Te-Er Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2505455857", "name": "Yu-Cong Sun"}, {"authorId": "2505800223", "name": "Zi-Jia Wang"}, {"authorId": "9365229", "name": "Yi-Ming Lu"}, {"authorId": "2279943298", "name": "Cheng-Chao Shen"}], "abstract": "Representation alignment speeds up diffusion transformer training by pulling an intermediate block of the model (student) toward features of a frozen pretrained encoder (teacher). Which teacher layer to align, and for how long, is still set by convention, and each alternative costs a training run. We find that alignment helps where the student cannot linearly recover the teacher's features, not where it already resembles them. Since a deep teacher layer is largely predictable from the one below, we isolate what each layer adds, its increment, and measure how much of it an unaligned student recovers. The student fills the teacher's hierarchy from the bottom up and stalls near the top, which we call hierarchy filling: even after 400K steps it recovers almost none of the deepest. The recoverability gap is the unrecovered share of an increment, read from one unaligned checkpoint. In short runs that each align one teacher layer at one block, the gap nearly reproduces their ranking by FID improvement, and CKA, a measure of feature similarity, largely reverses it. Representation Alignment and Recoverability Estimation (RARE) picks the teacher layer with the largest gap before training. During training, it tracks each token's remaining distance to that layer, the online counterpart of the gap, weights tokens by it, and phases out the loss once the average distance stops falling. With SiT-B/2 on ImageNet $256\\times256$, RARE reaches an FID of 18.02 without guidance and 4.46 with it, ahead of seven alignment baselines including REPA, iREPA and HASTE. It also trains in 14% fewer GPU-hours than iREPA. Its FID stays below iREPA's across model scales, teachers, datasets and backbones."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ed8e45b64bc9333f3803a6a1655d4edf3667069c", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-03436", "ArXiv": "2609.03436", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.03436", "CorpusId": 291678015}, "title": "It's the Problem, Not the Path: Budget and Difficulty Confounds in LLM Reasoning Trajectories", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03436, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2480029085", "name": "Yigit Utku Bulut"}], "abstract": "Reasoning traces of large language models are widely read as containing\"breakthrough\"moments and early-legible fates. Both readings rest on measurements missing a counterfactual control at the level of the claim; we supply both controls. First, a restart-controlled truncation probe separates when a solution fits the continuation budget from when a prefix carries value that fresh computation cannot buy, comparing per-anchor continuation solve rates against from-scratch restart curves at matched total generated-token budget. Applied to 178 problem-model cells (89 MATH problems x two small open models, an outcome-blind but difficulty-targeted cohort), exactly 1 of 178 cells survives as prefix-limited; restart dose-response separates a compute-starved model from a capability-limited one; and wherever the matched budget lies inside the restart grid, continuing the model's own prefix beats restarting (9 of 9) -- predominantly compute compression rather than expanded reachability. Second, a pre-registered, difficulty-controlled test finds no detectable outcome information in early-window internal signals beyond a problem-difficulty baseline, and two generation-free analyses of public corpora show why this control is needed: a trace-blind difficulty proxy reaches AUROC 0.873 on 192K DeepSeek-R1 generations -- inside the published probe range -- and a closely matched reconstruction of the closest published early-window positive recovers a comparable pooled result (0.849) while within problem it is statistically indistinguishable from chance at all ten anchors (0.496 at t=4); a post-hoc within-targeted probe finds only a small average residual, concentrated in three low-failure problems. High pooled probe AUROCs cannot by themselves establish within-attempt information; a question-only baseline or within-problem evaluation is required."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bcb34bf32ad411d2a762f8494a546787b34fb9b7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.01106", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-01106", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.01106", "CorpusId": 291602067}, "title": "Hints Help But Do They Teach? Evaluating Skills Transfer in Code Generation", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01106, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "15857648", "name": "W. Badr"}], "abstract": "When a hint turns a failing generated program into a passing one, does it provide missing information or merely steer the model toward a solution it could already produce? We test these hypotheses on HumanEval+ and MBPP+ using executable evaluation. For Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, adaptive relevant hints rescue 36 of 79 selected failures; an unrelated hint rescues 19, while eight unhinted samples solve 46 and recover 31 of the 36 relevant-hint rescues. Phi-3.5-mini shows the same pattern: relevant hints rescue 42 of 101 failures, an unrelated hint rescues 17, and unhinted sampling solves 57, including 36 of the 42 relevant-hint rescues. Because the hint conditions use different attempt budgets, these comparisons do not isolate a purely semantic effect. Mechanistic tests on Qwen identify a stable activation direction shared by relevant and unrelated hints. Persistently adding this direction yields 14 rescues and 18 regressions, with no detectable net accuracy gain; learned low-rank interventions have a positive but imprecise estimated effect. Full textual specifications solve 22 of 24 context-defined problems, versus 5-11 for tested virtual-KV prefixes. Post-generation hidden-state probes transfer across benchmarks, with pooled AUROC 0.806 and 0.780, but their top-one selection advantage over token confidence is statistically unresolved. Overall, relevant hints can rescue failures, but most rescued solutions are already reachable through ordinary sampling, and the internal interventions tested here do not establish task-general capability transfer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9dbda4ada1221e89cd9c18a187c565db7742212a", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2608-20670", "ArXiv": "2608.20670", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2608.20670", "CorpusId": 291316926}, "title": "Why2Speak: Faithful Reasoning for Abstaining Action Policies", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20670, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2433234423", "name": "Shreya Mendi"}, {"authorId": "145217268", "name": "Brinnae Bent"}], "abstract": "Many agentic systems must repeatedly choose between acting and abstaining, making faithful reasoning important for oversight: an explanation is useful only if it reflects the computation that produced the action. We study this problem through intervention timing in multi-party conversation, where an assistant must decide whether to speak or remain silent. This setting exposes class imbalance, asymmetric action costs, and the possibility that exposing reasoning changes the policy being audited. Using Qwen3-8B, decoded with or without chain-of-thought reasoning, we compare direct decision policies, reasoning policies, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning. We find a capability-auditability tradeoff: the strongest direct policy achieves higher quality but exposes no reasoning to inspect, while the reasoning policy provides a trace at the cost of lower performance, particularly recall of true intervention opportunities. Supervised fine-tuning either suppresses reasoning or preserves it without improving decision quality, while reinforcement learning also fails to improve the reasoning policy. We identify one mechanism underlying this failure: group relative objectives provide no learning signal on confidently wrong prompts when sampled rollouts all select the same action. Controlled activation probes and behavioral ablations show that standard faithfulness methods can overstate evidence that exposed reasoning reflects the underlying decision process. Probability-based metrics saturate under confident decisions, probes are vulnerable to class imbalance and textual leakage, and reasoning ablations can confound reasoning content with changes in inference mode. Together, these results show that exposing reasoning can change an agent's action policy rather than simply make it observable. We provide controls for evaluating reasoning-based oversight of agents that can act or abstain."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4753f70576ca295b764248179c6e94007961fc99", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2605.26045", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2605-26045", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2605.26045", "CorpusId": 288671284}, "title": "Confidence and Calibration of Activation Oracles for Reliable Interpretation of Language Model Internals", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.26045, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2119003687", "name": "Federico Torrielli"}, {"authorId": "2266016715", "name": "Peter Schneider-Kamp"}, {"authorId": "2393206291", "name": "Lukas Galke Poech"}], "abstract": "An activation oracle is a language model trained to convert the activations of another model into natural language. However, it is so far unclear to what extent these verbalizations are calibrated. Crucially, the oracle's verbalization come without any measure of confidence. Here, we compare five methods for estimating the confidence of an activation oracle. Our experiments cover four oracles from two model families (Qwen and Gemma, from 8B to 27B parameters), tested on the secret word task, whereby the oracle is supposed to recover a secret word, that has been fine-tuned into a model but does not appear in the model outputs. Our results with $6{,}000$ samples per method/oracle combination show that the best calibration depends on the availability of a hypothesis space or a calibration set: When an assumed auditor can list the possible answers in advance, the oracle should score each candidate answer directly. Scoring the candidates gives a higher accuracy on every oracle and a confidence that separates correct from incorrect answers better than any other method. When the oracle has to write its answer as free text, the agreement over twenty sampled answers gives the best calibrated confidence, which needs only five labeled secret words to be tuned. When a calibration set is available, a rescaled probability of the answer is as good as re-sampling, at the cost of one generation instead of twenty. Asking the oracle to write a number for its confidence, on the other side, gives no useful signal on any oracle. Code and the patched trainer are available at https://github.com/federicotorrielli/probabilistic_activation_oracles."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "454a44e9f36c8997bb0ff176d0bccc1ccecb95d9", "externalIds": {"CorpusId": 293888116}, "title": "The Trace Is Not the State A Counterfactual Test of What Controls Multi-Digit Addition", "venue": "", "year": null, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2429219148", "name": "Tanmay Patil"}], "abstract": null}}]}
